@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+# Kempe reconfiguration generator — arXiv:2112.02313
+
+## Profile and trust status
+
+| field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | **A — structural hardness** |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | graph |
+| certificate form | integer tuple (a sequence of `[vertex,color]` moves) |
+| intended intuition | search pruning by quotienting anchors that name the same bichromatic component |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+The local construction and verification gates pass, including 200,000 random candidates and five attacks over eight shipping seeds. The required external oracle claim is **not complete**: `harden.py` was run, but OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “key limit exceeded” on every redraw. The checked-in transcripts are the harness-written error evidence; they are not represented as model failures.
+
+## Problem family
+
+This module covers the native Kempe-reconfiguration objects in Bonamy, Delecroix, and Legrand-Duchesne, [“Kempe changes in degenerate graphs”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.02313). The solver receives a relabeled rectangular grid and two proper 3-colorings. A move chooses one current bichromatic connected component and swaps its two colors. The answer is an exact-length move sequence taking the start coloring to the target.
+
+Generation is by composition of identities: begin with a proper checkerboard coloring, scramble it using valid Kempe changes, sample the answer walk, and replay that walk to obtain the target. No endpoint instance is solved during certificate construction. `verify` ignores `inst["answer"]`; it recomputes every component and compares the final coloring exactly.
+
+## Why Track A is the honest claim
+
+Section 1 defines the objects, and Lemma 1.1 guarantees that all (k)-colorings of a (d)-degenerate graph are Kempe equivalent for the tight regime (k=d+1). The same discussion says the proof can produce exponentially long sequences and that a polynomial bound in the general degeneracy setting remains open. It also explicitly names rectangular grids as 2-degenerate graphs with unbounded treewidth.
+
+The shipping 10×10 grid uses (d=2,k=3), has treewidth 10, maximum degree 4, and average degree 3.6. Thus it is outside the paper’s three polynomial regimes: Theorem 0.1 needs maximum degree at most (k); Theorem 0.2 needs maximum average degree strictly below (k); and Theorem 0.3 needs treewidth at most (k-1). This is not an average-case theorem for the planted distribution. The evidence for that narrower distributional claim is G5/G6: the width-256 bidirectional component beam generated 1,371,118 successors over eight instances (171,389 mean), took 6.29 seconds total in the final run, and solved 0/8.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset with seed 3 renders this complete instance:
+
+```text
+Vertices: 1..9; colors: 1,2,3; graph: a relabeled 3 by 3 grid.
+Edges:
+[[1,2],[1,6],[1,8],[2,4],[3,6],[3,7],[3,8],[4,5],[4,8],[5,9],[7,9],[8,9]]
+Start:
+[1,2,1,1,3,2,2,3,1]
+Target:
+[3,1,3,3,2,1,1,2,3]
+Return exactly 2 Kempe moves as JSON [vertex,target_color] pairs.
+```
+
+The answer is `[[6,1],[5,2]]`; `verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping its last move gives `[[6,1]]`, which returns `(False, "too few moves: got 1, expected exactly 2")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by drawing the twelve edges and tracing the two changing bichromatic components.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | vertices | grid | moves | burn-in | minimum endpoint Hamming distance | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 9 | 3×3 | 2 | 18 | 2 | hand example |
+| easy | 36 | 6×6 | 16 | 144 | 20 | locally valid; oracle unavailable |
+| medium | 64 | 8×8 | 16 | 256 | 40 | locally valid; oracle unavailable |
+| hard | 100 | 10×10 | 16 | 400 | 64 | **shipping preset pending oracle evidence** |
+
+Escalation grows the grid and attack budget while keeping the answer at 16 moves. It returns `cap_bound` only beyond the 20×20 language bound, where the 300-operation intended-route cap becomes binding.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | final measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted certificates verified |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged prose/Markdown round-trip passed; garbage returned `None` |
+| G4 | 0 hits / 200,000 structure-aware dynamic samples; space (200^{16}) |
+| G5 | shipping sampled density 0/200,000; demo exact answer-string count 42; strongest attack 171,389 successors and 0.787 s mean |
+| G6 | outlier, forward greedy, reverse greedy, random-restart-32, and bidirectional beam all 0/8 |
+| G7 | 200-vertex instance built in 0.50 s and verified; candidate space increased |
+| G8 | 140/140 relabelings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 111 characters, 28 estimated tokens, 32 atoms, 225 intended operations |
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+The bare harness reached only the first `easy` rung. Four redraws—two `openai/gpt-5.6-terra` and two `x-ai/grok-4.6`—all returned HTTP 403 before producing a reply. Per the harness contract, errors do not consume attempts and are not failures to solve; consequently there is no `hardened` or `too_easy` verdict and no defensible shipping-level oracle table yet.
+
+| preset | seeds | completed attempts | solved | outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| easy | 1568406928, 767022162, 1888959166, 1581798901 | 0 | 0 | four HTTP 403 redraw errors; pool unreachable |
+
+## G9 diagnostic arms
+
+| arm | solved / completed attempts | result |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | pool unreachable |
+| structural hint | 0/0 | pool unreachable; transcript retained |
+| placebo hint | 0/0 | pool unreachable; transcript retained |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo difference is unavailable, not zero. The intended route on the sampled shipping instance uses 225 component selections/vertex recolorings, below the 300-operation cap; its answer uses 111 characters and 32 atomic integers.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2112_02313 as g
+
+params = g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **params)
+statement = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit instances with:
+
+```bash
+scripts/emit.sh 2112.02313 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The Track A claim is empirical for this planted distribution, not implied by Lemma 1.1 and not a proof of average-case hardness. G4 samples uniformly from the statement-visible sequence language: at each state it chooses one of (n) anchors and either other color. Multiple anchors can name the same Kempe component, so 0/200,000 is a density over answer strings, not over distinct state transitions; G6 separately searches the quotient by distinct components.
+
+The generator would become easier if the walk were shorter, if intermediate colorings were exposed, or if endpoint discrepancies became monotone under the planted moves. It filters the obvious greedy and restart leaks. The strongest tried method is a bounded bidirectional component beam, not exhaustive breadth-first search. No SAT/SMT encoding, meet-in-the-middle disk search, or full implementation of the potentially exponential Las Vergnas–Meyniel recursion was attempted. Finally, the four-vendor no-tool evidence and the hinted/placebo diagnostic remain missing until the OpenRouter key limit is restored; this directory should not be submitted as hardened before rerunning those scripts.
